{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act statutory grounds for eviction","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only on the statutory grounds in the Anti-Eviction Act, such as nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, damage, lease violations, retirement or conversion of the property, after serving the required Notice to Quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to most residential rentals, including single-family homes, mobile homes and park land, apartment buildings and complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-unit premises","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"Hotel, motel and guest-house guests and guests rented to a transient or seasonal tenant (continuing hotel and motel guests with no alternate residence are covered); a unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it.","coverage_quotes":["The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes.","This law may not apply to two - or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two (2) or fewer rental units.","Additionally, this law does not apply to a unit held in trust on behalf of a member of the immediate family, if that family member is developmentally disabled, and permanently occupies the dwelling unit."]},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel and guest-house guests and transient or seasonal tenants; units held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies them.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes.","interaction":"A rent increase relied on for an eviction for nonpayment must not be unconscionable and must comply with all other laws and municipal ordinances, including rent control.","relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant by self-help, such as padlocking, changing locks, shutting off utilities or removing belongings, without a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict a tenant after a judge issues a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Entry into a dwelling unit and removal of tenants without their consent or a court judgment; not permitted in New Jersey under any circumstances.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who illegally evicts may be liable for damages to the tenant; in a successful forcible entry and detainer action the court may award possession, damages, court costs and reasonable attorney fees; the landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"Only an officer of the court can legally physically evict a tenant, after a judge has issued a Warrant for Removal.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
